At spin one, the positive-Gram feasibility conditions for
$\mathcal R_1^{\mathcal A}$ in \cite[Lemma~10 and Appendix~B.2]{AGJLM}
also characterize $\mathcal Q_1^{\mathcal A}$.

Counterexamples exist for every $N\ge3$. For the distributions in
Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, $S_\alpha(q)>S_\alpha(p)$ holds exactly for
$0\le\alpha<\alpha_*$, where $\alpha_*>1$ is the unique root above one of
\begin{equation}
 (9/4)^{\alpha_*}+7\,4^{-\alpha_*}=4.
\end{equation}
Here $S_0$ is log support size and $S_1=\HH$;
numerically $\alpha_*\simeq1.262623$.
No dimension-independent additive correction repairs the Shannon bound.

For every normalized state, support matrix $R$ of rank $k$, and
admissible $Q',c$, one has
\begin{equation}
 \HH(p_{Q',c,R})\ \ge\ -\log\sum_{d,t}q_R(d,t)^2.
\end{equation}

Let $H$ be the minimal positive generating set of a numerical semigroup
$T$. A positive integer $M$ is admissible for $T$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
 M\in T,\qquad h<2M\ \hbox{and}\ 2M-h\in T\quad(h\in H).
\end{equation}
When these conditions hold, the set
\begin{equation}
 S_{\min}(T,M)=\{M\}\cup\{|M-h|:h\in H,\ h\ne M\}
\end{equation}
realizes $T$, has gcd one, and is contained in every jump set realizing
$T$ with maximum $M$. It is therefore the unique inclusion-minimal such
set and has the smallest possible number of jumps.

Every odd-length partition has $R(\lambda)>0$.
An even-length partition has $R(\lambda)\le0$, with equality precisely
when $\lambda_{2i-1}=\lambda_{2i}$ for every adjacent pair.
More precisely, if $o(\lambda')$ is the number of odd parts of the
conjugate, then $R(\lambda)=(-1)^{k-1}o(\lambda')$ for $k>0$.
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 C(m)=O(m)+\begin{cases}p(m/2),&m\text{ even},\\0,&m\text{ odd},\end{cases}
 \quad
 N(m)=E(m)-\begin{cases}p(m/2),&m\text{ even},\\0,&m\text{ odd}.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}

For $n$ equiprobable normalized pure states with Gram matrix
$G=(1-c)I+cJ\succeq0$, their optimal success equals
\begin{equation}
 F_n(c)=\frac{\bigl(\sqrt{1+(n-1)c}+(n-1)\sqrt{1-c}\bigr)^2}{n^2}.
\end{equation}
On $0\leq c\leq1$, this function is nonincreasing.

For coefficient-square distributions $p,q$ and $0\leq t\leq1$,
\begin{equation}
 P(tp+(1-t)q)\leq tP(p)+(1-t)P(q).
\end{equation}
Thus equal-error inputs have an average with no larger error; strict
improvement is not guaranteed.
